# The packaging contract asserts on the exact bytes of checked-in scripts and
# workflows, newlines included, so every working tree has to hold the same line
# endings as the index. Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which
# rewrites text files on checkout and would fail those assertions on the line
# endings alone rather than on anything the contract is about. Nothing here is
# built by a tool that requires CRLF: there is no `.bat`, `.cmd`, or `.ps1`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Artwork is matched by content heuristics anyway; saying so is cheaper than
# trusting them with a file whose first bytes could look like text.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary

.git_archival.txt export-subst
